<td id="S5.T1.2.1.9.7" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:1.0pt;padding-right:1.0pt;">59.8(0.9)</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.9.8" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:1.0pt;padding-right:1.0pt;"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.9.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">47.8</span>(0.5)</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.9.9" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:1.0pt;padding-right:1.0pt;"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.9.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">71.6</span>(1.1)</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.9.10" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:1.0pt;padding-right:1.0pt;"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.9.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">52.9</span>(1.4)</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.9.11" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:1.0pt;padding-right:1.0pt;"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.9.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">47.9</span>(1.3)</td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Evaluation Results on the Downstream Tasks</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The LLM is continuously trained for 10,000 steps on the selected data in each round. After completing five rounds of training, we evaluate the zero-shot performance of Pythia-410M/1B on various downstream tasks and report the average accuracy along with the standard error for each dataset(see Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.3 Bilevel Optimization for Proxy Model and Score Model ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ BLISS: A Lightweight Bilevel Influence Scoring Method for Data Selection in Language Model Pretraining" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Our algorithm consistently outperforms MATES and random selection methods across multiple tasks. For example on 410M setting, BLISS, compared with MATES, improves <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.4\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.4</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.4\%</annotation></semantics></math> on SciQ, <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.7\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.7</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.7\%</annotation></semantics></math> on ARC-E, <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.8\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.8</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.8\%</annotation></semantics></math> on LogiQA, <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.2\%</annotation></semantics></math> on HellaSwag, <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.4\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.4</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.4\%</annotation></semantics></math> on PIQA, <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.2\%</annotation></semantics></math> on WinoGrande, and <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.2\%</annotation></semantics></math> on average accuracy (see Table <a href="#A4.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ Appendix D Additional results ‣ BLISS: A Lightweight Bilevel Influence Scoring Method for Data Selection in Language Model Pretraining" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>). Additionally, Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 5.5 Computational Cost ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ BLISS: A Lightweight Bilevel Influence Scoring Method for Data Selection in Language Model Pretraining" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> presents the evaluation results in relation to pretraining FLOPs and training steps. BLISS consistently outperforms other baseline methods throughout the entire five-round pretraining process (with 10k steps per round). In particular, our method on 1B setting achieves a <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.7\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.7\times</annotation></semantics></math> speedup in reaching the same performance as MATES, further validating the effectiveness of our data selection approach.</p>
</div>
